<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Qaraqara-Charka Confederation</h1><p class="st-meta">1200 CE – 1460 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A confederation of Aymara-speaking peoples in the southern Bolivian highlands, renowned as warriors and later linked to the silver mines of Potosí.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the centuries after Tiwanaku's collapse, the Qaraqara and Charka formed a federation of peoples in the southern highlands of modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Bolivia</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucre">Chuquisaca</a>, around 1200. The federation included groups such as the Chuy and Chicha.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The confederation was organised in two halves led by the Qaraqara in the south and the Charka in the north, each under hereditary lords. It is known chiefly from a memorial written by its lords in 1582, which describes their service as soldiers for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a>. The peoples herded llamas, farmed tubers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, and worked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and other metals.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The confederation came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> rule around 1460 and served the Incas in war. After the Spanish conquest, its lands formed the core of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/royal-audiencia-of-charcas">Royal Audiencia of Charcas</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mountain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a>, discovered in 1545, lay in Qaraqara territory.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 1200 CE – 1460 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South America</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.58%;width:5.17%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1200 CE – 1460 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ayaviri</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Local cults</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aymara</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Bolivia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Charka, Charcas confederation, Qaraqara</dd></dl></aside></div>

